      "observation": "The example concerns a contaminant transported by fluid in a one-dimensional pipe with constant velocity ū; q(x,t) denotes its concentration or density."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1, (1.2)",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + ū q_x(x,t) = 0",
      "observation": "The contaminant satisfies the scalar advection equation in the example without source terms. The preceding discussion states that subscripts denote partial derivatives."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3, §1.1, (1.8)",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + f(q(x,t))_x = 0",
      "observation": "The source introduces a one-dimensional homogeneous conservation law and calls f(q) the flux function."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4, §1.1.1, (1.10)",
      "anchor": "for any two points x₁ and x₂",
      "observation": "The integral conservation law is d/dt ∫ from x₁ to x₂ q(x,t) dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t))."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4, paragraph immediately following (1.10)",
      "anchor": "Each component of q measures the density of some conserved quantity",
      "observation": "The integral represents the total amount of the corresponding quantity between the two endpoints; equation (1.10) allows that amount to change only through flux past the endpoints."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4, contaminant example following (1.10)",
      "anchor": "the total mass of the contaminant is conserved as it flows down the pipe ... f(q) = ūq",
      "observation": "The source ties the scalar advection equation (1.2) to conservation of contaminant mass during transport and identifies its linear flux as f(q) = ūq."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4, same contaminant paragraph",
      "anchor": "If the total mass of contaminant is not conserved, because of chemical reactions taking place, for example",
      "observation": "Failure of contaminant mass conservation requires source terms in the conservation law. Chemical reactions are an example of a cause; the source refers to §2.5, Chapter 17, and elsewhere for further description."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4, §1.1.2, opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth",
      "observation": "Deriving the differential conservation law (1.8) from the integral law (1.10) requires sufficient smoothness of q and f(q)."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5, continuation of §1.1.2",
      "anchor": "At a discontinuity in q ... does not hold in the classical sense",
      "observation": "The source distinguishes the classical differential equation from the more fundamental integral conservation law, which continues to hold at discontinuities."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "x and t are the spatial and temporal variables for the one-dimensional contaminant example [printed p. 1, paragraph preceding (1.2) and (1.2)].",
      "q(x,t) is the scalar concentration or density of contaminant [printed p. 1, paragraph preceding (1.2)].",
      "ū is the constant fluid velocity in the pipe [printed p. 1, paragraph preceding (1.2)].",
      "f is the flux function, specialized to f(q) = ūq for the contaminant example [printed p. 3, (1.8); printed p. 4, paragraph following (1.10)].",
